Zanotti&#x2010;Fregonara P, et&#xa0;al. Brain inflammation co&#x2010;localizes highly with tau in mild cognitive impairment due to early&#x2010;onset Alzheimer's disease. Brain. 2025;148:119&#x2010;132.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Therriault J, Pascoal TA, Lussier FZ, et&#xa0;al. Biomarker modeling of Alzheimer's disease using PET&#x2010;based Braak staging. Nature Aging. 2022;2:526&#x2010;535.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kreisl WC, Lyoo CH, Liow J&#x2010;S, et&#xa0;al. 11C&#x2010;PBR28 binding to translocator protein increases with progression of Alzheimer's disease. Neurobiol Aging. 2016;44:53&#x2010;61.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Hopp SC, Lin Y, Oakley D, et&#xa0;al. The role of microglia in processing and spreading of bioactive tau seeds in Alzheimer's disease. J Neuroinflammation. 2018;15:269.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Colton CA, Mott RT, Sharpe H, Xu Q, Van Nostrand WE, Vitek MP. Expression profiles for macrophage alternative activation genes in AD and in mouse models of AD. J Neuroinflammation. 2006;3:27.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Parbo P, Ismail R, Hansen KV, et&#xa0;al. Brain inflammation accompanies amyloid in the majority of mild cognitive impairment cases due to Alzheimer's disease. Brain. 2017;140:2002&#x2010;2011.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zou J, Tao S, Johnson A, et&#xa0;al. Microglial activation, but not tau pathology, is independently associated with amyloid positivity and memory impairment. Neurobiol Aging. 2020;85:11&#x2010;21.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Schaffer Aguzzoli C, Ferreira PCL, Povala G, et&#xa0;al. Neuropsychiatric symptoms and microglial activation in patients with Alzheimer disease. JAMA Network Open. 2023;6:e2345175.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Rowe JB, O'Brien JT, Aigbirhio FI, et&#xa0;al. Microglial activation and tau burden predict cognitive decline in Alzheimer's disease. Brain. 2020;143:1588&#x2010;1602.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Chaney A, Williams SR, Boutin H. In vivo molecular imaging of neuroinflammation in Alzheimer's disease. J Neurochem. 2019;149:438&#x2010;451.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">41795660</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>08</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>08</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1552-5279</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>22</Volume><Issue>3</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Alzheimer's &amp; dementia : the journal of the Alzheimer's Association</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Alzheimers Dement</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Hippocampal subfield thickness and shape analysis in examining the impact of TDP-43 in primary age-related tauopathy.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>e71267</StartPage><MedlinePgn>e71267</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/alz.71267</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Hippocampal subfields are vulnerable to the transactive response DNA-binding protein of 43&#xa0;kDa (TDP-43) and tau in primary age-related tauopathy (PART). Geometry-based morphometric analysis can improve the detection of structural changes in the hippocampus.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Forty-seven cases of autopsy-confirmed PART without TDP-43 and 19 cases of PART with TDP-43 underwent antemortem magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) hippocampal segmentation and shape analysis. A separate cohort of 16 younger healthy individuals (YHIs) was included as a reference group. Hippocampal shape was analyzed in Python with non-parametric cluster permutation testing.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">PART(TDP+) group&#xa0;exhibited thinner left subiculum and CA1, with curvature deformations in left CA1 and CA2/3, relative to PART(TDP-). Both PART groups showed right-sided predominance of thinning in presubiculum and subiculum, as well as curvature deformations in right presubiculum, subiculum, and CA1 when compared to YHIs.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Geometry-based analysis showed different patterns of hippocampal thinning and curvature associated with PART(TDP+) compared to PART(TDP-).</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026 The Author(s). Alzheimer's &amp; Dementia published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of Alzheimer's Association.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Youssef</LastName><ForeName>Hossam</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0000-8876-8038</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Neurology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gatto</LastName><ForeName>Rodolfo G</ForeName><Initials>RG</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-2170-6662</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Neurology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Petersen</LastName><ForeName>Ronald C</ForeName><Initials>RC</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Neurology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Reichard</LastName><ForeName>R Ross</ForeName><Initials>RR</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Laboratory Medicine and Pathology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Jack</LastName><ForeName>Clifford R</ForeName><Initials>CR</Initials><Suffix>Jr</Suffix><A