n mild cognitive impairment and Alzheimer disease. Neurology. 2022;99(24):e2648&#x2010;e2660.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Garyfallidis E, Brett M, Amirbekian B, et al. Dipy, a library for the analysis of diffusion MRI data. Front Neuroinform. 2014;8:8.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lin S, Lin X, Chen S, et al. Association of MRI indexes of the perivascular space network and cognitive impairment in patients with obstructive sleep apnea. Radiology. 2024;311(3):e232274.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Taoka T, Ito R, Nakamichi R, et al. Reproducibility of diffusion tensor image analysis along the perivascular space (DTI&#x2010;ALPS) for evaluating interstitial fluid diffusivity and glymphatic function: CHanges in Alps index on multiple conditiON acquIsition eXperiment (CHAMONIX) study. Jpn J Radiol. 2022;40(2):147&#x2010;158.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Gao M, Liu Z, Zang H, et al. A histopathologic correlation study evaluating glymphatic function in brain tumors by multi&#x2010;parametric MRI. Clin Cancer Res. 2024;30:4876&#x2010;4886.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Yu X, Yin X, Hong H, et al. Increased extracellular fluid is associated with white matter fiber degeneration in CADASIL: in vivo evidence from diffusion magnetic resonance imaging. Fluids Barriers CNS. 2021;18(1):29.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Qian X, Ji F, Ng KK, et al. Brain white matter extracellular free&#x2010;water increases are related to reduced neurocognitive function in systemic lupus erythematosus. Rheumatology. 2022;61(3):1166&#x2010;1174.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sun Y, Han X, Li Z, et al. Quantifying neuroinflammation within deep gray matter in small vessel disease using diffusion tensor based free&#x2010;water imaging: a longitudinal study. Front Aging Neurosci. 2024;16:1361436.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Seker&#x2010;Polat F, Pinarbasi Degirmenci N, Solaroglu I, Bagci&#x2010;Onder T. Tumor cell infiltration into the brain in glioblastoma: from mechanisms to clinical perspectives. Cancer. 2022;14(2):443.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Li Y, Wang J, Song S&#x2010;R, Lv S&#x2010;Q, Qin J&#x2010;h, Yu S&#x2010;C. Models for evaluating glioblastoma invasion along white matter tracts. Trends Biotechnol. 2023;42:293&#x2010;309.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Watkins S, Sontheimer H. Hydrodynamic cellular volume changes enable glioma cell invasion. J Neurosci. 2011;31(47):17250&#x2010;17259.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">41795159</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>08</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>08</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1099-1492</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>39</Volume><Issue>4</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Apr</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>NMR in biomedicine</Title><ISOAbbreviation>NMR Biomed</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Sensitivity of Literature <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow> </mml:msub> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {T}_1 $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> Mapping Methods to the Underlying Magnetization Transfer Parameters.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>e70246</StartPage><MedlinePgn>e70246</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/nbm.70246</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Magnetization transfer (MT) has been identified as the principal source of <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow> </mml:msub> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {T}_1 $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> variability in the MRI literature. This study assesses the sensitivity of established <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow> </mml:msub> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {T}_1 $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> mapping techniques to variations in the underlying MT parameters. For each <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow> </mml:msub> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {T}_1 $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> -mapping method, the observed <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow> </mml:msub> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {T}_1 $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> was simulated as a function of the underlying MT parameters <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msubsup><mml:mrow><mml:mi>p</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mtext>MT</mml:mtext></mml:mrow> </mml:msubsup> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {p}_i^{\mathrm{MT}} $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> , corresponding to different brain regions of interest (ROIs) at 3T. As measures of sensitivity, the derivatives <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics><mml:mrow><mml:mi>&#x2202;</mml:mi> <mml:msubsup><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mtext>observed</mml:mtext></mml:mrow> </mml:msubsup> <mml:mo>/</mml:mo> <mml:mi>&#x2202;</mml:mi> <mml:msubsup><mml:mrow><mml:mi>p</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mtext>MT</mml:mtext></mml:mrow> </mml:msubsup> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ \partial {T}_1^{\mathrm{observed}}/\partial {p}_i^{\mathrm{MT}} $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> were computed and analyzed with a linear mixed-effects model as a function of <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msubsup><mml:mrow><mml:mi>p</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mi>i</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mtext>MT</mml:mtext></mml:mrow> </mml:msubsup> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {p}_i^{\mathrm{MT}} $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> , ROI, pulse sequence type (e.g., inversion-recovery and variable-flip angle), and the individual sequences. The analyzed <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow> </mml:msub> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {T}_1 $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> -mapping sequences have a considerable sensitivity to changes in the semisolid spin pool size <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msubsup><mml:mrow><mml:mi>m</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mtext>s</mml:mtext></mml:mrow> </mml:msubsup> <mml:mo>,</mml:mo> <mml:mspace/> <mml:msubsup><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mtext>f</mml:mtext></mml:mrow> </mml:msubsup> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {m}_0^{\mathrm{s}},\kern0.3em {T}_1^{\mathrm{f}} $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> of the free, <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msubsup><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mtext>s</mml:mtext></mml:mrow> </mml:msubsup> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {T}_1^{\mathrm{s}} $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> of the semisolid spin pool, and the (inverse) exchange rate <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mtext>x</mml:mtext></mml:mrow> </mml:msub> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {T}_{\mathrm{x}} $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> . All derivatives vary considerably with the underlying MT parameters and between pulse sequences. In general, the derivatives cannot be determined by the sequence type but rather depend on the implementation details of the sequence. One notable exception is that variable-flip-angle methods are, in general, more sensitive to the exchange rate than inversion-recovery methods. Variations in the observed <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow> <mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow> <mml:mrow><mml:mn>1</mml:mn></mml:mrow> </mml:msub> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {T}_1 $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> can be caused by several underlying MT parameters, and the sensitivity to each parameter depends on both the underlying MT parameters and the sequence.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026 John Wiley &amp; Sons Ltd.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Assl&#xe4;nder</LastName><ForeName>Jakob</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-2288-038X</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Center for Biomedical Imaging, Department of Radiology, New York University School of Medicine, New York, New York, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Center for Advanced Imaging Innovation and Research (CAI2R), Department of Radiology, New York University School of Medicine, New York, New York, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>R01&#xa0;NS131948</GrantID><Agency>NIH/NINDS</Agency><Country/></Grant><Grant><GrantID>P41&#xa0;EB017183</GrantID><Agency>NIH/NIBIB</Agency><Country/></Grant></GrantList><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>NMR Biomed</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>8915233</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0952-3480</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D008279" MajorTopicYN="Y">Magnetic Resonance Imaging</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000379" MajorTopicYN="N">methods</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D006801" MajorTopicYN="N">Humans</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D001921" MajorTopicYN="N">Brain</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000000981" MajorTopicYN="N">diagnostic imaging</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D012680" MajorTopicYN="N">Sensitivity and Specificity</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000465" MajorTopicYN="N">Algorithms</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D015203" MajorTopicYN="N">Reproducibility of Results</DescriptorName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">MT</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">T1</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">magnetization transfer</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">parameter mapping</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">quantitative MRI</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">relaxometry</Keyword></KeywordList></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2025</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>18</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>23</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>22</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDat