ng study. Epilepsia. 2025;66.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhang H, Schneider T, Wheeler&#x2010;Kingshott CA, Alexander DC. NODDI: practical in&#xa0;vivo neurite orientation dispersion and density imaging of the human brain. Neuroimage. 2012;61(4):1000&#x2013;1016.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Owen TW, de Tisi J, Vos SB, Winston GP, Duncan JS, Wang Y, et&#xa0;al. Multivariate white matter alterations are associated with epilepsy duration. Eur J Neurosci. 2021;53(8):2788&#x2013;2803.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kozma C, Horsley J, Hall G, Simpson C, de Tisi J, Miserocchi A, et&#xa0;al. Combined impact of gray and superficial white matter abnormalities: implications for epilepsy surgery. Epilepsia. 2025;66:3688&#x2013;3699.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wood DA, Townend M, Guilhem E, Kafiabadi S, Hammam A, Wei Y, et&#xa0;al. Optimising brain age estimation through transfer learning: a suite of pre&#x2010;trained foundation models for improved performance and generalisability in a clinical setting. Hum Brain Mapp. 2024;45(4):e26625.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Iglesias JE, Billot B, Balbastre Y, Magdamo C, Arnold SE, Das S, et&#xa0;al. SynthSR: a public AI tool to turn heterogeneous clinical brain scans into high&#x2010;resolution T1&#x2010;weighted images for 3D morphometry. Sci Adv. 2023;9(5):eadd3607.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Mansour LS, Seguin C, Winkler AM, Noble S, Zalesky A. Topological cluster statistic (TCS): toward structural connectivity&#x2013;guided fMRI cluster enhancement. Netw Neurosci. 2024;8(3):902&#x2013;925.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Serin E, Zalesky A, Matory A, Walter H, Kruschwitz JD. NBS&#x2010;predict: a prediction&#x2010;based extension of the network&#x2010;based statistic. Neuroimage. 2021;244:118625.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">41793080</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>07</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>07</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1479-8301</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>26</Volume><Issue>2</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Psychogeriatrics : the official journal of the Japanese Psychogeriatric Society</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Psychogeriatrics</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Eligibility for Anti-Amyloid-&#x3b2; Monoclonal Antibodies in Patients With Primary Progressive Aphasia due to Alzheimer's Disease in Japan.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>e70152</StartPage><MedlinePgn>e70152</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/psyg.70152</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Primary progressive aphasia (PPA) is a clinical syndrome characterized by progressive language impairment. The logopenic variant (lvPPA) is frequently associated with Alzheimer's disease (AD) pathology. With the approval of anti-amyloid-&#x3b2; monoclonal antibodies, such as lecanemab and donanemab, for the treatment of AD, accurately differentiating lvPPA due to AD (lvAD) from the other PPA variants has become highly important.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirteen patients with PPA who underwent cognitive testing, including the Standard Language Test of Aphasia (SLTA), brain magnetic resonance imaging, single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT), cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) biomarker analysis, and amyloid positron emission tomography (PET) imaging were enrolled in this study. Patients were classified into the lvPPA, semantic variant PPA (svPPA), and nonfluent/agrammatic variant PPA (nfaPPA) groups based on the results of clinical and neuropsychological assessments. We determined the proportion of PPA patients in each group who met the optimal use guidelines for anti-amyloid monoclonal antibody therapy.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Six (86%) of the 7 lvPPA patients were amyloid positive (A+), and 5 (71%) were eligible for lecanemab or donanemab. In most lvPPA patients, SPECT displayed an AD-typical pattern of hypoperfusion in the bilateral temporoparietal regions, posterior cingulate gyrus, and precuneus. One lvPPA patient was negative for AD biomarkers and demonstrated an atypical perfusion pattern for AD, and was subsequently rediagnosed as having frontotemporal lobar degeneration.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These results suggest that SPECT may play a supportive role in differentiating lvAD from PPA. In the current clinical era of anti-amyloid-&#x3b2; monoclonal antibody therapy, accurate identification of lvAD among patients with language-predominant symptoms is increasingly important. Larger studies should be performed in the near future to validate these findings.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026 The Author(s). Psychogeriatrics published by John Wiley &amp; Sons Australia, Ltd on behalf of Japanese Psychogeriatric Society.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Inagawa</LastName><ForeName>Shoya</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Geriatric Medicine, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Inagawa</LastName><ForeName>Yuta</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Geriatric Medicine, Tokyo Medical University, Tokyo, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Tsugawa</LastName><ForeName>Akito</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Geriatric Medicine, Tokyo Medical Universit