<Citation>Kuhlman JJ, Alhaj Moustafa M, Gupta V, Jiang L, Tun HW (2021) Primary cauda equina lymphoma treated with CNS-centric approach: a case report and literature review. J Blood Med 12:645&#x2013;652</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.2147/JBM.S325264</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">34321945</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">8312505</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lapolla P, Maiola V, Familiari P, Tomei G, Gangemi D, Ienzi S et al (2024) Primary cauda equina lymphoma mimicking meningioma. J Clin Med. https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm13164959</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3390/jcm13164959</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">39201102</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">11355680</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sasaki R, Ohta Y, Yamada Y, Tadokoro K, Takahashi Y, Sato K et al (2018) Neurolymphomatosis in the cauda equina diagnosed by an open biopsy. Intern Med 57(23):3463&#x2013;3465</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.2169/internalmedicine.1049-18</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">30101916</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">6306530</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Suzuki K, Yasuda T, Hiraiwa T, Kanamori M, Kimura T, Kawaguchi Y (2018) Primary cauda equina lymphoma diagnosed by nerve biopsy: a case report and literature review. Oncol Lett 16(1):623&#x2013;631</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">29928449</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">6006481</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Boruah DK, Gogoi P, Gogoi BB, Sarma K, Sarma G, Hazarika K et al (2025) Magnetic resonance imaging spectrum of cauda equina and conus lymphoma: keys to unravel the differential diagnosis with a review of the literature. South Asian J Cancer 14(3):646&#x2013;656</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1055/s-0043-1771406</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">41445907</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41832350</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>15</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2399-3642</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month><Day>14</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Communications biology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Commun Biol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Thalamic dynamics orchestrate the recovery of tonic alertness during nocturnal sleep inertia.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/s42003-026-09839-w</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>At dawn, we experience a shift from slumber to sentience following neurophysiological transitions, termed as sleep inertia (SI). Although resting-state fMRI studies have discovered brain reorganizations during SI, the neural basis underlying impaired alertness in SI remains unclear. We conduct simultaneous EEG-fMRI recordings on 26 adults with repeated measures of pre-sleep, nocturnal sleep and consecutive post-sleep awakenings. Using the psychomotor vigilance task (PVT) to probe tonic alertness, we discover that cingulo-opercular network (CON) activation, inclusive of thalamus, troughs upon awakening, and increments along with the post-sleep awake duration. The dynamic recovery of thalamus activity during SI depends on prior sleep architecture and the awake duration, mediating the PVT performances on awakening. Although CON connectivity remains stable, the connectivity changes between thalamus and frontoparietal network (FPN) are associated with changes of thalamic activation and PVT performances during SI. Collectively, thalamic activity and its coupling with the FPN support the restoration of tonic alertness during SI, providing a concise framework for the neural mechanisms underlying cognitive recovery upon awakening.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026. The Author(s).</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chen</LastName><ForeName>Shuo</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>McConnell Brain Imaging Center, Montreal Neurological Institute, McGill University, Montr&#xe9;al, QC, Canada.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Biomedical Sciences and Engineering, National Central University, Taoyuan, Taiwan, ROC.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kung</LastName><ForeName>Yi-Chia</ForeName><Initials>YC</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-9295-7560</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Radiology, Tri-Service General Hospital, National Defense Medical University, Taipei, Taiwan, ROC.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hsiao</LastName><ForeName>Fan-Chi</ForeName><Initials>FC</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-8559-9707</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Counseling and Industrial/Organizational Psychology, Ming Chuan University, Taoyuan, Taiwan, ROC.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lee</LastName><ForeName>Hsin-Chien</ForeName><Initials>HC</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-7557-8259</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Graduate Institute of Medical Humanities, Taipei Medical University, Taipei, Taiwan, ROC.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Research Center of Sleep Medicine, Taipei Medical University Hospital, Taipei, Taiwan, ROC.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Psychiatry, Taipei Medical University Hospital, Taipei, Taiwan, ROC.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chen</LastName><ForeName>Chun-Chuan</ForeName><Initials>CC</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Biomedical Scie