h Parkinson disease. Human Brain Map. 42, 4399&#x2013;4421 (2021).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1002/hbm.25556</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Cieslak, M. et al. QSIPrep: an integrative platform for preprocessing and reconstructing diffusion MRI data. Nat. Methods 18, 775&#x2013;778 (2021).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">34155395</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">8596781</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41592-021-01185-5</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ghosh, S. S. et al. Nipype: a flexible, lightweight and extensible neuroimaging data processing framework in Python. Front. Neuroinformat. https://doi.org/10.3389/fninf.2011.00013 (2011).</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Garyfallidis, E. et al. Dipy, a library for the analysis of diffusion MRI data. Front. Neuroinformat. https://doi.org/10.3389/fninf.2014.00008 (2014).</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bakdash, J. Z. &amp; Marusich, L. R. Repeated measures correlation. Front. Psychol. 8, 456 (2017).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">28439244</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">5383908</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3389/fpsyg.2017.00456</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Shrout, P. E. &amp; Fleiss, J. L. Intraclass correlations: uses in assessing rater reliability. Psychol. Bull. 86, 420&#x2013;428 (1979).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">18839484</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1037/0033-2909.86.2.420</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41826661</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>14</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1476-5543</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month><Day>13</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Journal of perinatology : official journal of the California Perinatal Association</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Perinatol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Morphometric alterations of the gray matter of the frontal lobe during childhood in healthy children born prematurely. Repercussions of foetal and postnatal growth restriction.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/s41372-026-02618-9</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND/OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine, in healthy children born prematurely with very low birth weight (VLBW), whether the cerebral morphometry of the frontal lobe is affected by foetal growth restriction (FGR) and extrauterine growth restriction (EUGR).</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS/METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A magnetic resonance imaging study was performed of 117 children during childhood within a cohort of VLBW infants. A neuropsychological study was performed using the WISC-V test and the Child Behaviour Checklist scale.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In children with FGR and EUGR, the volume of the lateral orbitofrontal cortex was reduced in both hemispheres. In this group of children, we observed smaller volumes of the pars opercularis in the right hemisphere.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We can conclude that FGR with EUGR in VLBW infants is associated with smaller volumes and surface area of the lateral orbitofrontal cortex in both hemispheres. These nutritional situations do not condition relevant structural modifications in the rest of the studied structures of the frontal lobe in our sample.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026. The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature America, Inc.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Uberos</LastName><ForeName>J</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-3519-6678</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Paediatrics; School of Medicine; Granada University, Granada, Spain. juberos@ugr.es.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neonatal intensive care unit. San Cecilio Clinical Hospital. Medicine Faculty, Granada, Spain. juberos@ugr.es.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Instituto de Investigaci&#xf3;n Biosanitaria (ibs.GRANADA), Health Sciences Technological Park, Granada, Spain. juberos@ugr.es.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Carrasco-Solis</LastName><ForeName>M</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neuropaediatrics Unit. Paediatrics Service. San Cecilio Clinical Hospital, Granada, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Laynez-Rubio</LastName><ForeName>C</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neuropaediatrics Unit. Paediatrics Service. San Cecilio Clinical Hospital, Granada, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Nieto-Ruiz</LastName><ForeName>A</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Instituto de Investigaci&#xf3;n Biosanitaria (ibs.GRANADA), Health Sciences Technological Park, Granada, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Behavioural Science Methodology. Faculty of Psychology. University of Granada, Granada, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>EURISTIKOS Excellence Centre for Paediatric Research, Biomedical Research Centre. Department of Paediatrics. Faculty of Medicine. University of Granada, Granada, Spain.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ruiz-L&#xf3;pez</LastName><ForeName>A</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-6170-3119</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Neonata