Gait-Carr E, Connolly DJA, King D (2017) Moyamoya syndrome associated with hereditary spherocytosis: an emerging clinical entity. J Pediatr Hematol Oncol 39(3):233&#x2013;234</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1097/MPH.0000000000000789</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">28221268</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Yadegari S, Aminian A (2017) Management in rare association of moyamoya syndrome and hereditary spherocytosis. Minerva Pediatr 69(4):368&#x2013;369</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.23736/S0026-4946.16.04427-3</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">28612582</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Tokunaga Y et al (2001) Moyamoya syndrome with spherocytosis: effect of splenectomy on strokes. Pediatr Neurol 25(1):75&#x2013;7</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/S0887-8994(01)00283-1</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">11483402</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Umesh SU et al (2018) Moyamoya disease associated with hereditary spherocytosis. Neurol India 66(4):1174&#x2013;1175</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.4103/0028-3886.237016</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">30038116</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sun Y et al (2024) Moyamoya syndrome secondary to hereditary spherocytosis: a case report. Asian J Surg 47(9):4241&#x2013;4242</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.asjsur.2024.05.089</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">38816272</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kossorotoff M (2012) Cognitive decline in moyamoya: influence of chronic cerebral hypoxia, history of stroke, or comorbid conditions? Dev Med Child Neurol 54(1):5&#x2013;6</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1111/j.1469-8749.2011.04159.x</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">22171927</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Dobson SR et al (2002) Moyamoya syndrome in childhood sickle cell disease: a predictive factor for recurrent cerebrovascular events. Blood 99(9):3144&#x2013;3150</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1182/blood.V99.9.3144</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">11964276</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Iolascon A et al (2017) Recommendations regarding splenectomy in hereditary hemolytic anemias. Haematologica 102(8):1304&#x2013;1313</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3324/haematol.2016.161166</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">28550188</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">5541865</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Huq S et al (2010) Hereditary spherocytosis. J Health Popul Nutr 28(1):107&#x2013;9</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">20214092</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">2975852</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Guzman R et al (2009) Clinical outcome after 450 revascularization procedures for moyamoya disease. Clinical article. J Neurosurg 111(5):927&#x2013;935</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3171/2009.4.JNS081649</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">19463046</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Matsushima T et al (1992) Surgical treatment of moyamoya disease in pediatric patients&#x2013;comparison between the results of indirect and direct revascularization procedures. Neurosurgery 31(3):401&#x2013;405</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1227/00006123-199209000-00003</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">1407421</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Rother RP et al (2005) The clinical sequelae of intravascular hemolysis and extracellular plasma hemoglobin: a novel mechanism of human disease. JAMA 293(13):1653&#x2013;1662</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1001/jama.293.13.1653</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">15811985</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Morris CR (2005) Dysregulated arginine metabolism, hemolysis-associated pulmonary hypertension, and mortality in sickle cell disease. JAMA 294(1):81&#x2013;90</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1001/jama.294.1.81</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">15998894</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">2065861</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41879732</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>25</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1546-3141</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month><Day>25</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>AJR. American journal of roentgenology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>AJR Am J Roentgenol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Breast MRI in Patients With Active Implanted Metallic Devices: How We Do It.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2214/AJR.26.34661</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Given increases in both breast MRI utilization and the prevalence of active implanted metallic devices (AIMDs), radiology practices are expected to encounter growing number of referrals for breast MRI in patients with AIMDs. Under MRI, AIMDs can experience forces and torques, vibrations, heating, and electrical interference or malfunction. AIMDs also distort the magnetic field, causing signal voids, signal pile-up, geometric distortion, and fat suppression failure. Yet limited guidance exists on safely performing breast MRI in this patient population. This Perspective presents approaches for safely performing breast MRI in patients with AIMDs while maintaining diagnostic integrity. Key components include multistep screening for early identification of AIMDs, adherence to vendor MRI-conditional guidelines, and protocol modification techniques to meet these guidelines while balancing image quality, scan time, and artifact mitigation. Illustrative clinical cases demonstrate breast MRI examinations performed safely in patients with various AIMDs including a cardiac pacemaker, cardiac loop recorder, sacral nerve stimulator, responsive neurostimulator, hypoglossal nerve stimulator, spinal cord stimulator, and deep brain stimulator. Situations where MRI is inadvisable and alternative modalities may be preferred are also considered. New MRI safety CPT codes are also highlighted. Implementing the described workflows can expand safe access to breast MRI for patients with AIMDs.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Sanders</LastName><ForeName>Jeremiah</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Radiology, Division of Medical Physics, Mayo Clinic, Phoenix, AZ.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Panda</LastName><ForeName>Anshuman</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Radiology, Division of Medical Physics, Mayo Clinic, Phoenix, AZ.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Harper</LastName><ForeName>Laura</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Radiology, Division of Breast Imaging and Intervention, Mayo Clinic, Phoenix, AZ.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Patel</LastName><ForeName>Bhavika</ForeName><Initials>B</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Radiology, Division of Breast Imaging and Intervention, Mayo Clinic, Phoenix, AZ.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Sharpe</LastName><ForeName>Richard</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Radiology, Division of Breast Imaging and Intervention, Mayo Clinic, Phoenix, AZ.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>25</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>AJR Am J Roentgenol</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>7708173</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0361-803X</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>25</Day><Hour>12</Hour><Minute>48</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>25</Day><Hour>12</Hour><Minute>48</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>25</Day><Hour>11</Hour><Minute>12</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>aheadofprint</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">41879732</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.2214/AJR.26.34661</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41879295</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>25</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1469-994X</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month><Day>25</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Nicotine &amp; tobacco research : official journal of the Society for Research on Nicotine and Tobacco</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Nicotine Tob Res</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>White Matter Microstructure and Hemispheric Asymmetry Alterations in Adults Who Smoke: A Connectome-Based Analysis.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">ntag064</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/ntr/ntag064</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Cigarette smoking poses a pervasive health risk increasingly recognized for its impact on the nervous system, yet its effects on brain structure and organization remain incompletely understood. This study examined how smoking addiction influences white matter microstructure and the architecture of structural brain networks in adults.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We used diffusion tensor imaging to compare fractional anisotropy between participants who smoke (n = 122) and non-smoking control participants (n = 55) to detect white matter abnormalities. Whole-brain structural connectomes were constructed, with connections categorized by fiber length. Network features quantified included degree centrality and hemispheric asymmetry. Adaptive Best-Subset Selection method was applied to identify white matter connections that may serve as biomarkers for distinguishing individuals who smoke from those who do not.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Participants who smoke showed widespread white matter alterations in pathways related to reward valuation, habit formation, and craving, along with abnormalities in both short- and medium-range connections. Reduced rightward asymmetry in the cuneus indicated lateralized structural changes possibly linked to cue processing in nicotine dependence. Discriminative connections involved orbitofrontal, sensorimotor, and visual regions, underscoring how smoking reshapes brain networks supporting reward valuation, motor control, and cue reactivity.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Nicotine dependence is associated with extensive alterations in white matter microstructure and network organization, including reduced hemispheric asymmetry. These findings suggest that smoking affects structural connectivity in circuits involved in emotional regulation and reward-related memory.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; The Author(s) 2026. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of the Society for Research on Nicotine and Tobacco. All rights reserved. For commercial re-use, please contact reprints@oup.com for reprints and translation rights for reprints. All other permissions can be obtained through our RightsLink service via the Permissions link on the article page on our site&#x2014;for further information please contact journals.permissions@oup.com.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhang</LastName><ForeName>Hanlu</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Statistics and Finance, School of Management, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei 230000, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Mathematics and Statistics, Hunan Normal University, Changsha 410006, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Shi</LastName><ForeName>Yuanyuan</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Magnetic resonance department, The First Affiliated Hospital of Henan Polytechnic University, Jiaozuo 454000, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Second People's Hospital of Jiaozuo, Jiaozuo 454000, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Li</LastName><ForeName>Caixia</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Magnetic resonance department, The First Affiliated Hospital of Henan Polytechnic University, Jiaozuo 454000, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Second People's Hospital of Jiaozuo, Jiaozuo 454000, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhao</LastName><ForeName>Wei</ForeName><Initials>W</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-1098-3229</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Mathematics and Statistics, Hunan Normal University, Changsha 410006, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of Applied Statistics and Data Science, Hunan Normal University, College of Hunan Province, Changsha 410006, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>25</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>Nicotine Tob Res</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>9815751</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>1462-2203</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>8</Month><Day>3</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2026</Year><Month>2</Month><Day>19</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>19</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>25</Day><Hour>12</Hour><Minute>48</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>25</Day><Hour>12</Hour><Minute>48</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>25</Day><Hour>8</Hour><Minute>54</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>aheadofprint</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">41879295</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1093/ntr/ntag064</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">8540215</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">41879269</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>25</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>25</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1573-2665</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>49</Volume><Issue>2</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Journal of inherited metabolic disease</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Inherit Metab Dis</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Clinical and Genetic Characteristics of Free Sialic Acid Storage Disorder.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>e70148</StartPage><MedlinePgn>e70148</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/jimd.70148</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Free sialic acid storage disorder (FSASD) is a lysosomal storage disorder that results from biallelic pathogenic variants in the SLC17A5 gene. This gene codes for sialin, a 12-transmembrane domain protein that exports the charged sugar N-acetylneuraminic acid (Neu5Ac; sialic acid) out of the lysosome. Dysfunctional sialin causes accumulation of free sialic acid within lysosomes and a range of clinical manifestations, such as intellectual disability, facial dysmorphisms, and increased urinary excretion of free sialic acid. These findings, along with characteristic brain abnormalities on MRI, make the diagnosis of FSASD. Despite recognition of the clinical and imaging phenotype, the natural history of FSASD has not been extensively elucidated. Therefore, we prospectively characterized the clinical, molecular, laboratory, and imaging findings of eight children with FSASD in order to pursue biomarker discovery with collaborators in a consortium of FSASD investigators. Our cohort displayed a high prevalence of ophthalmologic and auditory abnormalities, including myopia, exotropia, and abnormal ABR. Prominent features include impaired CNS myelination, a very thin corpus callosum, documentation of varying levels of intellectual disability, elevated urine, plasma, and CSF free sialic acid levels, and essentially normal endocrine, hematologic, and immunologic parameters. The consistent finding of delayed but progressive myelination suggests that quantitative assessment of myelination by MRI and <sup>1</sup>H MRS should be added to the list of potential clinical outcome measures for future clinical trials.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026 The Author(s). Journal of Inherited Metabolic Disease published by John Wiley &amp; Sons Ltd on behalf of SSIEM.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wolfenson</LastName><ForeName>Zoe</ForeName><Initials>Z</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0008-2207-5755</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>NIH Undiagnosed Diseases Program, National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, Maryland, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Grois</LastName><ForeName>Gabriella</ForeName><Initials>G</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>NIH Undiagnosed Diseases Program, National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, Maryland, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hailemeskel</LastName><ForeName>Ruth F</ForeName><Initials>RF</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>NIH Undiagnosed Diseases Program, National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, Maryland, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Sabaii</LastName><ForeName>Marla</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>NIH Undiagnosed Diseases Program, National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, Maryland, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Huryn</LastName><ForeName>Laryssa A</ForeName><Initials>LA</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-0309-9419</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Eye Institute, NIH, Bethesda, Maryland, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zein</LastName><ForeName>Wadih M</ForeName><Initials>WM</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-3771-6120</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Eye Institute, NIH, Bethesda, Ma