 doi: 10.1055/s-0030-1262437,</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1055/s-0030-1262437</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">20648407</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Standring S. Gray&#x2019;s anatomy: the anatomical basis of clinical practice. 41st edn. Philadelphia: Elsevier Limited; (2016).</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Barnett MED, Lam LM. Management of carotid artery dissection. Curr Treat Options Cardio Med. (2025) 27:9. doi: 10.1007/s11936-024-01064-0</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1007/s11936-024-01064-0</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Debette S, Mazighi M, Bijlenga P, Pezzini A, Koga M, Bersano A, et al. ESO guideline for the management of extracranial and intracranial artery dissection. Eur Stroke J. (2021) 6:XXXIX&#x2013;LXXXVIII. doi: 10.1177/23969873211046475</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1177/23969873211046475</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC8564160</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">34746432</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lindsay FW, Mullin D, Keefe MA. Subacute hypoglossal nerve paresis with internal carotid artery dissection. Laryngoscope. (2003) 113:1530&#x2013;1533. doi: 10.1097/00005537-200309000-00022</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1097/00005537-200309000-00022</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">12972929</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41877735</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>25</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>25</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic-eCollection"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2632-6140</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>8</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>BMJ neurology open</Title><ISOAbbreviation>BMJ Neurol Open</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Exploring transient global amnesia: a 14-year retrospective study of 142 cases.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>e001354</StartPage><MedlinePgn>e001354</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">e001354</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bmjno-2025-001354</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Transient global amnesia (TGA) provides a unique perspective into the relationship between stress and memory formation. This study examines the clinical and diagnostic profiles of TGA and its mimics and reviews the current evidence to enhance understanding of its elusive pathophysiology.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">We retrospectively reviewed 142 consecutive acute amnesia cases (January 2011 to July 2024) at a tertiary centre in Sydney. Cases were identified via diagnostic codes and confirmed through manual review. Patients were classified as either TGA or non-TGA according to established criteria. Clinical data were extracted from electronic medical records. Acute-phase brain MRI was performed in 83% of TGA cases, with all scans independently reviewed by both authors. Additional investigations, including cardiac studies, EEG and cerebrospinal fluid analysis, were evaluated when available.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Among 142 cases, 69% were TGA and 31% non-TGA, with non-TGA cases demonstrating a broader age range and male predominance (68%). The study identified triggers beyond the commonly recognised physical and emotional stressors. Wake-up TGA occurred in 12.0%, which is higher than previously reported. Repetitive questioning was a highly specific diagnostic feature (sensitivity 63.3%, specificity 93.2%). Brief episodes under 1&#x2009;hour occurred in 8% of TGA cases. MRI was most informative at 24-48&#x2009;hours after symptom onset, with 93.3% showing typical hippocampal punctate diffusion lesions, which were lateralised more frequently to the left (51.3%) than to the right (33.3%). A 'TGA plus' subgroup (2.4%) was characterised by extrahippocampal punctate diffusion lesions. Common mimics included transient epileptic amnesia, syncope, head injury and drug-induced amnesia.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The study expands the clinical spectrum of TGA, identifying various precipitating triggers, including wake-up amnesia. A proposed mechanism of catecholamine-mediated hippocampal dysfunction is discussed. Collectively, these findings highlight the interplay of stress, arousal and hippocampal susceptibility, warranting prospective studies incorporating catecholamine measurement.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; Author(s) (or their employer(s)) 2026. Re-use permitted under CC BY-NC. No commercial re-use. See rights and permissions. Published by BMJ Group.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Xi</LastName><ForeName>Shuo</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-4951-1624</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Neurology, Westmead Hospital, Westmead, New South Wales, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Martin</LastName><ForeName>Andrew J</ForeName><Initials>AJ</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Neurology, Blacktown Hospital, Blacktown, New South Wales, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>18</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>BMJ Neurol Open</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>101775450</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>2632-6140</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">AMNESIA</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">COGNITION</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">MEMORY</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>There are no competing interests.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>6</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>2</Month><Day>19</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>25</Day><Hour>7</Hour><Minute>10</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>25</Day><Hour>7</Hour><Minute>9</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>25</Day><Hour>4</Hour><Minute>24</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pmc-release"><Year>2026</Year><Month>3</Month><Day>18</Day></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>epublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">41877735</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC13007091</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1136/bmjno-2025-001354</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">bmjno-2025-001354</ArticleId></ArticleIdList><ReferenceList><Reference><Citation>Arena JE, Rabinstein AA. Transient global amnesia. Mayo Clin Proc. 2015;90:264&#x2013;72. doi: 10.1016/j.mayocp.2014.12.001.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.mayocp.2014.12.001</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">25659242</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Quick S, Speiser U, Richter N, et al. Transient global amnesia and broken heart syndrome: two faces of one pathology. Clin Auton Res. 2015;25:189&#x2013;91. doi: 10.1007/s10286-015-0284-z.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1007/s10286-015-0284-z</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">25822581</ArticleId></Article