chemical shifts and coupling constants for brain metabolites. NMR Biomed. 2000;13(3):129&#x2013;53.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Van den Vanhamme A, Van Huffel S. Improved method for accurate and efficient quantification of MRS data with use of prior knowledge. J Magn Reson. 1997;129:35&#x2013;43.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Chen X, Li J, Chen D, Zhou Y, Tu Z, Lin M, et al. CloudBrain-MRS: An intelligent cloud computing platform for in vivo magnetic resonance spectroscopy preprocessing, quantification, and analysis. J Magn Reson. 2023;358:107601.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhou Y, Wu Y, Su Y, Li J, Cai J, You Y, et al. Cloud-magnetic resonance imaging system: In the era of 6G and artificial intelligence. Magn Reson Lett. 2024:200138.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Guo D, Li S, Liu J, Tu Z, Qiu T, Xu J, et al. CloudBrain-NMR: An intelligent cloud-computing platform for NMR spectroscopy processing, reconstruction, and analysis. IEEE Trans Instrum Meas. 2024;73:1&#x2013;11.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bland JM, Altman DG. Measuring agreement in method comparison studies. Stat Methods Med Res. 1999;8:135&#x2013;60.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Baeshen A, Wyss PO, Henning A, O&#x2019;Gorman RL, Piccirelli M, Kollias S, et al. Test-retest reliability of the brain metabolites GABA and Glx with JPRESS, PRESS, and MEGA-PRESS MRS sequences in vivo at 3T. J Magn Reson Imaging. 2019;51:1181&#x2013;91.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kim JW, Lee CH, Yang Z, Kim BH, Lee YS, Kim KA. The spectrum of magnetic resonance imaging proton density fat fraction (MRI-PDFF), magnetic resonance spectroscopy (MRS), and two different histopathologic methods (artificial intelligence vs. pathologist) in quantifying hepatic steatosis. Quant Imaging Med Surg. 2022;12(11):5251&#x2013;62.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Rackayova V, Cudalbu C, Pouwels PJW, Braissant O. Creatine in the central nervous system: From magnetic resonance spectroscopy to creatine deficiencies. Anal Biochem. 2016;529:144&#x2013;57.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Marja&#x144;ska M, Deelchand DK, Kreis R. Results and interpretation of a fitting challenge for MR spectroscopy set up by the MRS study group of ISMRM. Magn Reson Med. 2021;87(1):11&#x2013;32.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Horn DI, Yu C, Steiner J, Buchmann J, Kaufmann J, Osoba A, et al. Glutamatergic and resting state functional connectivity correlates of severity in major depression - the role of pregenual anterior cingulate cortex and anterior insula. Front Syst Neurosci. 2010;4:33.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Noskov&#xe1; E, Fajnerov&#xe1; I, Pajuelo D, Stopkov&#xe1; P, Hor&#xe1;&#x10d;ek J. <sup>1</sup>H-MRS of the anterior cingulate cortex in obsessive-compulsive disorder: metabolic abnormalities in pgACC - controlled study. Eur Psychiat. 2023;66(S1):S105&#x2013;06.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Giavarina D. Understanding Bland Altman analysis. Biochem Med. 2015;25(2):141&#x2013;51.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services (CMS),Department of Health and Human Services (HHS). Clinical laboratory improvement amendments (CLIA) proficiency testing related to analytes and acceptable performance; correction. Fed Reg. 2022;87(221):68912.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Osorio-Garcia MI, Sima DM, Nielsen FU, Himmelreich U, Van Huffel S. Quantification of magnetic resonance spectroscopy signals with lineshape estimation. J Chemometrics. 2011;25(4):183&#x2013;92.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Mescher M, Merkle H, Kirsch J, Garwood M, Gruetter R. Simultaneousin vivo spectral editing and water suppression. NMR Biomed. 1998;11(6):266&#x2013;72.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Endres D, van Tebartz L, Meyer SA, Feige B, Nickel K, Bubl A, et al. Glutathione metabolism in the prefrontal brain of adults with high-functioning autism spectrum disorder: an MRS study. Mol Autism. 2017;8:10.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>An L, Araneta MF, Johnson C, Shen J. Simultaneous measurement of glutamate, glutamine, GABA, and glutathione by spectral editing without subtraction. Magn Reson Med. 2018;80(5):1776&#x2013;86.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Papanastasiou G, Drigas A, Skianis C, Lytras M, Papanastasiou E. Patient-centric ICTs based healthcare for students with learning, physical and/or sensory disabilities. Telematics Inform. 2017;35(4):654&#x2013;64.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41876923</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>25</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1522-2594</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month><Day>24</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Magnetic resonance in medicine</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Magn Reson Med</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>A Hybrid RF Coil for Whole-Brain Imaging in Non-Human Primates at 7&#x2009;T.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/mrm.70361</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Ultra-high field MRI (&#x2265;&#x2009;7&#x2009;T) offers unprecedented potential for mapping non-human primate (NHP) brain function. However, complex electromagnetic systems are required to meet the challenges of UHF imaging and the mechanical demands of awake NHP studies. To address these challenges, we developed a hybrid RF coil system optimized for whole-brain fMRI of macaques at 7&#x2009;T.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The hybrid coil system combines a 6-channel preamplifier-decoupled transceive dipole array with a 16-channel loop receive array, housed in a compact structure designed for awake imaging that maintains open visual fields. Key design features include compatibility with sphinx-position monkey chairs and head fixation systems required for awake experiments. Electromagnetic simulations guided dipole design to optimize uniform transmit performance and deep penetration. Phantom and in vivo experiments validated these predictions using anesthetized macaques and evaluated the system's readiness for awake imaging.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The hybrid coil demonstrated uniform <mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"> <mml:semantics> <mml:mrow><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>B</mml:mi> <mml:mn>1</mml:mn> <mml:mo>+</mml:mo></mml:msubsup> </mml:mrow> <mml:annotation>$$ {B}_1^{+} $$</mml:annotation></mml:semantics> </mml:math> distribution with phase-shimmed transmission across the brain. It supported robust parallel imaging, enabling up to R&#x2009;=&#x2009;3&#x2009;&#xd7;&#x2009;2 acceleration factors for high-resolution acquisitions. The achieved whole-brain tSNR in fMRI is comparable to that of an existing 8-TX/24-RX coil designed for imaging anesthetized macaques. Critically, submillimeter (0.75&#x2009;mm isotropic) resting-state fMRI revealed clear default-mode network connectivity, confirming the system's capability for high-quality functional imaging.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">By effectively addressing both the technical challenges of ultra-high field MRI and the mechanical constraints associated with visual stimulation in awake NHPs, this hybrid coil system provides a powerful tool for advancing our understanding of primate brain function.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026 The Author(s). Magnetic Resonance in Medicine published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of International Society for Magnetic Resonance in Medicine.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Djaballah</LastName><ForeName>Elias</ForeName><Initials>E</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Cognitive Neuroimaging Unit, INSERM, CEA, Universit&#xe9; Paris-Saclay, NeuroSpin Center, GIF-SUR-YVETTE, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>BAOBAB, Universit&#xe9; Paris-Saclay, CEA/Joliot/NeuroSpin, GIF-SUR-YVETTE, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Giacomini</LastName><ForeName>&#xc9;ric</ForeName><Initials>&#xc9;</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>BAOBAB, Universit&#xe9; Paris-Saclay, CEA/Joliot/NeuroSpin, GIF-SUR-YVETTE, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gapais</LastName><ForeName>Paul-Fran&#xe7;ois</ForeName><Initials>PF</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-6592-5034</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>BAOBAB, Universit&#xe9; Paris-Saclay, CEA/Joliot/NeuroSpin, GIF-SUR-YVETTE, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Luong</LastName><ForeName>Michel</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0000-7527-5867</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Universit&#xe9; Paris-Saclay, CEA/DRF/IRFU/DACM, GIF-SUR-YVETTE, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Amadon</LastName><ForeName>Alexis</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-2667-9387</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>BAOBAB, Universit&#xe9; Paris-Saclay, CEA/Joliot/NeuroSpin, GIF-SUR-YVETTE, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhu</LastName><ForeName>Qi</ForeName><Initials>Q</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-0993-3400</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Cognitive Neuroimaging Unit, INSERM, CEA, Universit&#xe9; Paris-Saclay, NeuroSpin Center, GIF-SUR-YVETTE, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><Agency>The French Alternativ